# Cache policy.
#
# A good part of the 6 August session was spent looking at a build from an hour
# earlier: "collapsing isn't working for some reason" turned out to be a cached
# copy of app.js, and neither ctrl+shift+R nor ctrl+F5 dislodged it reliably.
# That is not a browser quirk to work around at the keyboard — it is a cache
# header. GitHub Pages serves max-age=600 and there is no way to change it,
# which is one more reason the site now lives on Cloudflare.
#
# no-cache does NOT mean "do not cache". It means "cache it, but revalidate
# before use", so a repeat visit costs one conditional request and gets a 304
# with no body when nothing has changed. The page stays fast and can never be
# stale. Stated explicitly rather than relying on the platform default, so it
# survives a move to a host whose default is worse.
#
# Rules in this file ACCUMULATE: two rules matching the same path both apply and
# their Cache-Control values are concatenated into one nonsensical header. So
# the paths below must not overlap, and images and data are deliberately left to
# the platform default rather than given a second, conflicting rule.

/*.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# The JavaScript now sits in three non-overlapping trees: /app/js the
# application, /lib the shared modules both it and the pages use, /js the
# scripts belonging to the tutorial and sample pages.
/app/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/lib/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/js/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/css/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache
